          %center
            %div{:style => "margin:-40px 10px 10px 10px;"}
              %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/AdSCFTForSmallN-230627.jpg", :width => "750"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              (graphics adapted from
              = succeed ")" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/Anyonic+topological+order+in+TED+K-theory"} SS22
          %p
            Notice that for real-world applications such as to the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
            = succeed "-problem" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+gap"} mass gap
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chromodynamics"} quantum chromodynamics
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            typically is indeed small (the number of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/color+charge"} colors
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chromodynamics"} quantum chromodynamics
            is
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mi c
                    %mo =
                    %mn 3
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_c = 3
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            = succeed "-corrections" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FQCD+correspondence"} AdS/QCD correspondence
            are going to be crucial for the full discussion of these applications:
          %center
            %a{:href => "/schreiber/files/Schreiber-MTheoryMathematics2020-v200126.pdf#page=8"}
              %img{:src => "/schreiber/files/ProblemQCDToProblemM230120.jpg", :width => "670"}/
          %p
            In lack of a full formulation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
            (see
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "M-theory#TheOpenProblem"} M-theory – The open problem
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+invariance"} conformal invariance
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large+N+limit"} large N limit
            and/or exact
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+spacetime"} anti de Sitter geometry
            are being argued to be of use for understanding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+chromodynamics"} quantum chromodynamics
            (for instance the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quark-gluon+plasma"} quark-gluon plasma
            (
            %a{:href => "#PolicastroSonStarinets01"}> Policastro-Son-Starinets 01
            , but most notably
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confined
            = succeed "-spectra" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hadron"} hadron
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FQCD+correspondence"} AdS/QCD correspondence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/solid+state+physics"} solid state physics
            (the
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-CFT+in+condensed+matter+physics"} AdS-CFT in condensed matter physics
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#HartnollLucasSachdev16"} Hartnoll-Lucas-Sachdev 16
          %p
            More in detail, since the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/near+horizon+geometry"} near horizon geometry
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BPS+state"} BPS
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/black+branes"} black branes
            is conformal to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartesian+product"} Cartesian product
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+spaces"} anti de Sitter spaces
            with the unit
            = succeed "-sphere" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"} cosmology
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+D-brane+models"} intersecting D-brane models
            realizes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+boundary"} asymptotic boundary
            of an
            %em approximately
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+spacetime"} anti de Sitter spacetime
            (see for instance
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Kaloper04"} Kaloper 04
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#FlachiMinamitsuji09"} Flachi-Minamitsuji 09
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Randall-Sundrum+models"} Randall-Sundrum models
            %em warped throat
            geometries, see
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Uranga05"} Uranga 05, section 18
          %p
            These warped throat models go back to
            %a{:href => "#KlebanovStrassler00"} Klebanov-Strassler 00
            which discusses aspects of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confinement
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            on conincident ordinary and
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fractional+D-brane"} fractional
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D3-branes"} D3-branes
            at the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularity"} singularity
            of a warped
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conifold"} conifold
            %a{:href => "#KlebanovWitten98"} Klebanov-Witten 98
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/KlebanovStrassler.jpg", :width => "640"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              snippet grabbed from
              %a{:href => "#Uranga05"} Uranga 05, section 18
            %p
              here: “RS”=
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Randall-Sundrum+model"}> Randall-Sundrum model
              ; “KS”=
              %a{:href => "#KlebanovStrassler00"} Klebanov-Strassler 00
          %p
            In particular this means that AdS-CFT duality applies in
            %em some approximation
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+D-brane+models"} intersecting D-brane models
            (e.g.
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Soda10"} Soda 10
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#GHMO16"} GHMO 16
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+effects"} non-perturbative effects
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            on the intersecting branes in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gravity"} gravity
            on the ambient warped throat
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+spacetime"} AdS
            (
            %a{:href => "#KlebanovStrassler00"}> Klebanov-Strassler 00, section 6
            )
          %h3#matching_single_trace_observables_to_string_excitation Matching single trace observables to string excitation
          %p
            The
            = succeed "/observables" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/single+trace+operators"} single trace operators
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theories"} conformal field theories
            such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theories"} super Yang-Mills theories
            play a special role in the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-CFT+correspondence"} AdS-CFT correspondence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
            excitations on the
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS+spacetime"} AdS
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            side of the correspondence, where, curiously, the “string of characters/letters” in the argument of the trace gets literally mapped to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            (see the references
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#ReferencesRelationToStringExcitations"} below
          %p
            From
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Polyakov02"} Polyakov 02
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/single+trace+operators"} single trace operators
            as
            %em letter
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %em words
          %blockquote
            %p The picture which slowly arises from the above considerations is that of the space-time gradually disappearing in the regions of large curvature. The natural description in this case is provided by a gauge theory in which the basic objects are the texts formed from the gauge-invariant words. The theory provides us with the expectation values assigned to the various texts, words and sentences.
          %blockquote
            %p These expectation values can be calculated either from the gauge theory or from the strongly coupled 2d sigma model. The coupling in this model is proportional to the target space curvature. This target space can be interpreted as a usual continuous space-time only when the curvature is small. As we increase the coupling, this interpretation becomes more and more fuzzy and finally completely meaningless.
          %p
            From
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BerensteinMaldacenaNastase02"} Berenstein-Maldacena-Nastase 02
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
            for the elementary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+observables"} field observables
            (“letters”)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi Φ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{\Phi}
            above:
          %blockquote
            %p
              In summary, the “string of
              = succeed "s\u201D" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Z
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
              carries one unit of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi J
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} J
              which is one unit of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mo +
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_+
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+bit+model"} string bit model
              where each bit is a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Z
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z
              operator.
          %p
            On the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/CFT"} CFT
            side these
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BMN+operators"} BMN operators
            of fixed length (of “letters”) are usefully identified as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+chains"} spin chains
            which, with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dilatation+operator"} dilatation operator
            regarded as their
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hamiltonian"} Hamiltonian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integrable+systems"} integrable systems
            (
            %a{:href => "#MinahanZarembo02"}> Minahan-Zarembo 02
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BeisertStaudacher03"} Beisert-Staudacher 03
          %p This integrability allows a detailed matching between
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/single+trace+operators"}> single trace operators
                \/
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/BMN+operators"} BMN operators
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D%3D4+N%3D4+super+Yang-Mills+theory"} D=4 N=4 super Yang-Mills theory
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+field+theory"} classical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+superstring"} Green-Schwarz superstring
                on
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+spacetime"} AdS5
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ×
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/5-sphere"} S5
          %p
            under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+duality"} AdS/CFT duality
            (
            %a{:href => "#BeisertFrolovStaudacherTseytlin03"}> Beisert-Frolov-Staudacher-Tseytlin 03
            , …). For review see
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BBGK04"} BBGK 04
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#BeisertEtAl10"} Beisert et al. 10
          %p (…)
          %p
            %br/
          %div
            %h2#PolyakovGaugeStringDualitySection Polyakov gauge/string duality
            %p
              Key ideas underlying what is now known as
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-CFT+correspondence"} holographic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duality+in+string+theory"} duality in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
              and specifically as
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-QCD+correspondence"} holographic QCD
              (see also at
              = succeed ")" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+light+front+QCD"} holographic light front QCD
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Polyakov"} Alexander Polyakov
              (cf. historical reminiscences in
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "#Polyakov08"} Polyakov (2008)
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge%2Fstring+duality"} gauge/string duality
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confined
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
              (the
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mass+gap+problem"} mass gap problem
              = succeed "-" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/color+charge"} color
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
              (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+lines"}> Wilson lines
              ) between
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
              as dynamical
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
            %p
              The logic here proceeds in the following steps (cf.
              %a{:href => "#Polyakov07"} Polyakov (2007), §1
              and see the commentary
              = succeed "):" do
                %a{:href => "#NoticeTheDecisiveInsight"} below
            .float_right_image{:style => "margin: 0px 0px 10px 15px"}
              %figure{:style => "margin: 0 0 0 0"}
                %img{:src => "/nlab/files/HadronicStringInPionScattering.jpg", :width => "400px"}/
                %figcaption{:style => "text-align: center"}
                  From
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "string+theory#Veneziano12"} Veneziano 2012
            %ol
              %li
                %p
                  %strong flux tubes confine as dynamical strings
                %p
                  The starting point is the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hypothesis"} hypothesis
                  that the strong coupling of particles (such as
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quarks"} quarks
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
                  (such as the
                  = succeed ")" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+nuclear+force"} strong nuclear force
                  = succeed "\u201D" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
                  = succeed "\u201D)" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wilson+lines"} Wilson lines
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
                  with a given
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tension"} tension
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#KogutSusskind74"} Kogut &amp; Susskind (1974)
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a{:href => "#KogutSusskind75"} (1975)
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a{:href => "#Wilson74"} Wilson (1974)
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#Polyakov79"} Polyakov (1979)
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#Polyakov80"} (1980)
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a{:href => "#Polyakov80"} (1987)
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#MakeenkoMigdal81"} Makeenko &amp; Migdal (1981)
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "Nambu-Goto+action#Nambu70"} Nambu (1970)
                  %a{:href => "Nambu-Goto+action#Gotō71"} Gotō (1971)
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                %p Under this assumption it would be:
                %ol
                  %li
                    %p
                      the
                      = succeed "/" do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tube"} flux tube
                      = succeed "\u2018s" do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tension"} tension
                      which keeps the particles at theirs endpoints
                      = succeed "," do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/confinement"} confined
                  %li
                    %p
                      the excitation of these
                      = succeed "/" do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
                      which follow
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Regge+trajectories"} Regge trajectories
                      (such as of
                      = succeed ");" do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hadrons"} hadrons
                  %li
                    %p
                      the
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering"} scattering
                      of these
                      = succeed "/" do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
                      which explains the observed
                      = succeed "," do
                        %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Veneziano+amplitudes"} Veneziano amplitudes
                %p which are the main qualitative features to be explained.
              %li#QuantumFluxTubesProbeHigherDim
                %p
                  %strong quantum flux tubes probe effective higher dimensions
                %p
                  But if so, famous quantum effects make such
                  = succeed "/" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
                  behave like propagating in an effective/emergent higher-dimensional spacetime:
                %ul
                  %li
                    either because one regards the string as
                    %em a priori
                    propagating in non-critical
                    = succeed "-" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dimension"} dimension
                    = succeed "," do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mn 4
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 4
                    = succeed "/" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dilaton+field"} dilaton field
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Liouville+field"} Liouville field
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi ϕ
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
                    appears to cancel the
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} quantum anomaly
                    and acts like a warped 5th dimension:
                %p
                  this was the point of view published in
                  %a{:href => "#Polyakov81"} Polyakov (1981)
                  but later claimed, in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#Polyakov08"} Polyakov (2008)
                %ul
                  %li
                    or because one regards the string as propagating in critical dimension (10 for the
                    = succeed ")" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superstring"} superstring
                %p
                  which is the point of view adopted later in
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-CFT+duality"} AdS-CFT duality
                  %a{:href => "AdS-CFT+correspondence#Maldacena97a"} Maldacena (1997)
                %p
                  with only the endpoints of the
                  = succeed "/" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tube"} flux tube
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string"} string
                  constrained to lie in the original lower dimensional spacetime
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#Polyakov98"} Polyakov (1998)
                  %a{:href => "#Polyakov99"} (1999)
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
                %p
                  which now appears (in modern language that Polyakov did not originally use) as a “
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/brane"}> brane
                  ” inside a higher dimensional
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bulk"} bulk
                  spacetime.
                %blockquote
                  %p
                    Notice that in this picture the observable physics that we set out to describe takes place
                    %em on the brane
                    (
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/underlying"} underlying
                    which is typically flat
                    = succeed "!)" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+boundary"} asymptotic
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
                    of a higher-dimensional
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bulk"} bulk
                    spacetime, while the (potentially large) extra dimensions of a possibly
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mo ∼
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
                    = succeed "-bulk" do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS"} AdS
                    %em thickness
                    of the
                    = succeed "," do
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tube"} flux tube
                    = succeed "cf." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
                    %a{:href => "#Polyakov08"} Polyakov (2008), p. 3
                    = succeed "." do
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
              %li
                %p
                  %strong
                    large/small
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi N
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                    confined gauge theory is holographic string theory/M-theory
                %p
                  Thus the description of strongly coupled matter via
                  = succeed "/" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
                  now reveals a
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holography"} holographic
                  situation where strongly-coupled quantum fields on
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+brane+models"} intersecting branes
                  are equivalently described by a theory of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
                  mediated by
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
                  propagating in a higher dimensional
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bulk"} bulk
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
                %p
                  In relation to gauge string duality this is due to
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a{:href => "#GubserKlebanovPolyakov98"} Gubser, Klebanov &amp; Polyakov (1998)
                  = succeed "," do
                    %em
                      %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS%2FCFT+duality"} AdS/CFT duality
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a{:href => "#PolyakovRychkov00"} Polyakov &amp; Rychkov (2000)
            %blockquote
              %p
                While this dual bulk string theory is itself strongly-coupled unless the “number of coincident branes” is humongous (the “
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/large-N+limit"}> large-N limit
                ”) and thus unrealistic after all, the difference is that recognizing the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/branes"} branes
                as physical objects reveals a web of concrete hints as to the string’s strongly-coupled (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"}> non-perturbative
                ) completion, going under the working title
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a{:href => "AdS-CFT+correspondence#SmallNCorrections"}
                      AdS-CFT – Small
                      %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                        %semantics
                          %mrow
                            %mi N
                          %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
                      corrections
              %p
                In summary, the plausible approach of understanding strongly-coupled quantum gauge theories by regarding their
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
                as dynamical
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
                seems to recast the
                %a{:href => "mass+gap#ReferencesMassGapProblem"} Millennium Problem
                of understanding strongly-coupled matter into the problem of formulating
                = succeed ":" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-theory"} M-theory
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+brane+models"} intersecting brane models
                of single (or a small number of
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coincident+branes"} coincident
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M-branes"} M-branes
                (such as the
                %em
                  %a{:href => "AdS-QCD+correspondence#WittenSakaiSugimotoModel"} Witten-Sakai-Sugimoto model
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
                system) on whose
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
                the desired strongly-coupled field theory is realized (such as
                = succeed ")." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
            %p#NoticeTheDecisiveInsight
              Notice the decisive early insight of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Polyakov"} Alexander Polyakov
              here: While the idea that
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
              somehow describe
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hadron"} hadronic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bound+states"} bound states
              was the very origin of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
              in the early 1970s (“dual resonance models”), the mainstream
              %em abandoned
              this perspective in the later 1970s when the critical dimension and the full spectrum of the string became fully understood (cf.
              = succeed ")" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Goddard-Thorn+no-ghost+theorem"} Goddard-Thorn no-ghost theorem
              %em instead
              string should be understood as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/GUT"} grand unified
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/theory+of+everything"} theory of everything
              including
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+gravity"} quantum gravity
              (see e.g. the historical review of
              %a{:href => "string+theory#Veneziano12"} Veneziano (2012), esp. pp. 30-31
              which still clings to this perspective). From here it was only through the long detour of first discovering, inside this grander theory:
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-branes"} D-branes
              (and
              = succeed ")" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-branes"} M5-branes
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/near-horizon+geometry"} near-horizon
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS-CFT+duality"} AdS-CFT duality
              just before the 2000s and then another decade of exploring
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+D-brane+models"} intersecting D-brane models
              that the community in the 2010s came back full circle to Polyakov’s holographic perspective on
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/QCD"} QCD
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holographic+QCD"} holographic QCD
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strings"} strings
              are
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux+tubes"} flux tubes
              that propagate not (alone) in the observable 4 spacetime dimensions but in a primarily unobservable (meanwhile known as
              = succeed "-like)" do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Randall-Sundrum+model"} Randall-Sundrum
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bulk"} bulk
              spacetime – a holographic description of reality that
              %a{:href => "#Polyakov99"} Polyakov (1999)
              referred to as the
              = succeed "," do
                %em wall of the cave
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Plato"} Plato
              (cf. also
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "#Polyakov08"} Polyakov (2008), p. 6
            %p
              Our whereabouts in this remarkable picture are still often misunderstood today: If string theory is a theory of nature, then, it seems, we see the wall but not the cave: we live on a
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∼
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowskian
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intersecting+D-brane+model"} brane intersection
              at the (
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+boundary"}> asympotic
              )
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary"} boundary
              of a primarily unobserved
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∼
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sim
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+spacetime"} anti de Sitter
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bulk"} bulk
              – which may better be thought of not as physical space but as a configuration space of quantum flux.
          %h2#Checks Checks
          %p
            At the heart of the duality is the observation that the classical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action+functionals"} action functionals
            for various
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            coupled to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Einstein+gravity"} Einstein gravity
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+spacetime"} anti de Sitter spacetime
            are, when expressed as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functions"} functions
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+boundary"} asymptotic boundary
            values of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generating+functions"} generating functions
            for the
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/correlators"} correlators
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/n-point+functions"} n-point functions
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            on that asymptotic boundary.
          %p
            These computations were laid out in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Witten98"} Witten 98, section 2.4 “Some sample computation”
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+geometry"} differential geometry
            (involving neither
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry"} supersymmetry
            nor
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            %a{:href => "#HartnollLucasSachdev16"} Hartnoll-Lucas-Sachdev 16, Section 1.6
          %p
            For the more ambitious matching of the spectrum of the dilatation operator of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/N%3D4+D%3D4+super+Yang-Mills+theory"} N=4 D=4 super Yang-Mills theory
            to the corresponding spectrum of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+superstring"} Green-Schwarz superstring
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+anti+de+Sitter+spacetime"} super anti de Sitter spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi AdS
                    %mn 5
                  %mo ×
                  %msup
                    %mi S
                    %mn 5
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdS_5 \times S^5
            detailed checks are summarized in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#BeisertEtAl10"} Beisert et al. 10
            %a{:href => "#Escobedo12"} Escobedo 12
          %center
            %img{:src => "https://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/AdSCFTFrolovTseytlinLimitCheck.jpg", :width => "600"}/
          %blockquote
            %p
              graphics grabbed from
              %a{:href => "#Escobedo12"} Escobedo 12
          %p
            Comparison to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+scattering+amplitudes"} string scattering amplitudes
            beyond the planar SCFT limit:
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#ABP18"} ABP 18
          %p
            Numerical checks using
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lattice+gauge+theory"} lattice gauge theory
            are reviewed in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Joseph15"} Joseph 15
          %p
            Exact duality checks pertaining to the full stringy regime for
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/AdS3-CFT2"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi AdS
                        %mn 3
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %msub
                        %mi CFT
                        %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdS_3/CFT_2
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#EberhardtGaberdiel19a"} Eberhardt-Gaberdiel 19a
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#EberhardtGaberdiel19b"} Eberhardt-Gaberdiel 19b
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#EberhardtGaberdielGopakumar19"} Eberhardt-Gaberdiel-Gopakumar 19
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "AdS3-CFT2+and+CS-WZW+correspondence#ReferencesAdS3CFT2"} there
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              Umut Gursoy, Guim Planella Planas,
              %em Worldsheet from worldline
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.10142"}> arXiv:2311.10142
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            The solutions to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity"} supergravity
            that preserve the maximum of 32
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetries"} supersymmetries
            are (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "BPS+state#HEGKS08"} HEGKS 08 (1.1)
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi AdS
                        %mn 5
                      %mo ×
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 5
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdS_5 \times S^5
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+supergravity"} type II supergravity
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi AdS
                        %mn 7
                      %mo ×
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 4
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdS_7 \times S^4
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi AdS
                        %mn 4
                      %mo ×
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mn 7
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdS_4 \times S^7
                in
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
          %p
            as well as their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            and plane wave limits. These are the main
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KK-compactifications"} KK-compactifications
            for the following examples-
          %h3#AdS5CFT4
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi AdS
                    %mn 5
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi CFT
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdS_5 / CFT_4
            – Horizon limit of D3-branes
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
            on 5d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+spacetime"} anti de Sitter spacetime
            (times a 5-sphere) is dual to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/N%3D4+D%3D4+super+Yang-Mills+theory"} N=4 D=4 super Yang-Mills theory
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D3-brane"} D3-brane
            at the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/asymptotic+boundary"} asymptotic boundary
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#Maldacena97a"}> Maldacena 97, section 2
            )
          %p
            (
            %a{:href => "#AharonyGubserMaldacenaOoguriOz99"}> Aharony-Gubser-Maldacena-Ooguri-Oz 99, section 3 and 4
            )
          %h3#AdS7CFT6
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi AdS
                    %mn 7
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi CFT
                    %mn 6
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdS_7 / CFT_6
            – Horizon limit of M5-branes
          %p
            We list some of the conjectured statements and their evidence concerning the case of
            = succeed "-duality." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi AdS
                      %mn 7
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                    %msub
                      %mi CFT
                      %mn 6
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} AdS_7/CFT_6
          %p
            The hypothesis of
            %a{:href => "#Maldacena97a"} Maldacena 1997, section 3.1
            (for review see (
            %a{:href => "#AharonyGubserMaldacenaOoguriOz99"}> Aharony, Gubser, Maldacena, Ooguri &amp; Oz 1999, section 6.1.1
            ) is that
          %ul
            %li
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/6d+%282%2C0%29-superconformal+QFT"} 6d (2,0)-superconformal QFT
              on the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              coincident
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-branes"} M5-branes
          %p is holographically related to
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kaluza-Klein+mechanism"} reduced on
              the 4-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sphere"} sphere
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi S
                      %mn 4
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^4
              with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              units of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flux"} flux
              of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
              to
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/7d+supergravity"} 7d supergravity
              on an asymptotically
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+spacetime"} anti de Sitter spacetime
          %p In
          %ul
            %li#WittenI
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
              ,
              %em Five-Brane Effective Action In M-Theory
              J. Geom. Phys.22:103-133,1997 (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9610234"}> arXiv:hep-th/9610234
              )
          %p
            effectively this relation was already used to computed the 5-brane
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            in the abelian case from the states of abelian
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/7d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 7d Chern-Simons theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            necessary to make this come out right is what led to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quadratic+Functions+in+Geometry%2C+Topology%2C+and+M-Theory"} Hopkins-Singer 02
            and hence to the further mathematical development of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            and its application in physics.)
          %p
            In (
            %a{:href => "#Witten98"}> Witten 98, section 4
            ) this construction is argued for from within the framework of AdS/CFT, explicitly identifying the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/7d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 7d Chern-Simons theory
            here with the compactification of the 11-dimensional Chern-Simons term of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/11-dimensional+supergravity"} 11-dimensional supergravity
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi S
                            %mrow
                              %mn 11
                              %mi d
                              %mi SUGRA
                              %mo ,
                              %mi CS
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi C
                            %mn 3
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi AdS
                                %mn 7
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mi S
                                %mn 4
                          %msub
                            %mi C
                            %mn 3
                          %mo ∧
                          %msub
                            %mi G
                            %mn 4
                          %mo ∧
                          %msub
                            %mi G
                            %mn 4
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mi N
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi AdS
                                %mn 7
                          %msub
                            %mi C
                            %mn 3
                          %mo ∧
                          %msub
                            %mi G
                            %mn 4
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  S_{11d SUGRA, CS}(C_3)
                  \&amp;=
                  \\int_{AdS_7} \int_{S^4} C_3 \wedge G_4 \wedge G_4
                  \\\
                  \&amp; = N \, \int_{AdS_7} C_3 \wedge G_4
                  \\end{aligned}
                  \\,.
          %p
            But in fact the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+anomaly"} quantum anomaly
            cancellation (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+mechanism"}> GS-type mechanism
            ) for 11d sugra introduces a quantum correction to this Chern-Simons term (
            %a{:href => "\\DLM"}> DLM, equation (3.14)
            ), making it locally become
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi S
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ω
                          %mo ,
                          %msub
                            %mi C
                            %mn 3
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi AdS
                                %mn 7
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mrow
                              %msup
                                %mi S
                                %mn 4
                          %msub
                            %mi C
                            %mn 3
                          %mo ∧
                          %msub
                            %mi G
                            %mn 4
                          %mo ∧
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi G
                            %mn 4
                          %mo +
                          %msub
                            %mi I
                            %mn 8
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ω
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mi N
                          %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                          %msub
                            %mo ∫
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi AdS
                                %mn 7
                          %mrow
                            %mo (
                            %msub
                              %mi C
                              %mn 3
                            %mo ∧
                            %msub
                              %mi G
                              %mn 4
                            %mo +
                            %mfrac
                              %mn 1
                              %mn 48
                            %msub
                              %mi CS
                              %mrow
                                %msub
                                  %mi p
                                  %mn 2
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi ω
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo −
                            %mfrac
                              %mn 1
                              %mn 12
                            %msub
                              %mi CS
                              %mrow
                                %mfrac
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mn 2
                                %msub
                                  %mi p
                                  %mn 1
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi ω
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ∧
                            %mi tr
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mi F
                              %mi ω
                            %mo ∧
                            %mi ω
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  S(\omega,C_3)
                  \&amp;=
                  \\int_{AdS_7} \int_{S^4} C_3 \wedge G_4 \wedge (G_4 + I_8(\omega))
                  \\\
                  \&amp; = N \, \int_{AdS_7}
                  \\left(
                  C_3 \wedge G_4
                  +
                  \\frac{1}{48} CS_{p_2}(\omega) -
                  \\frac{1}{12} CS_{\frac{1}{2}p_1}(\omega)
                  \\wedge tr(F_\omega \wedge \omega)
                  \\right)
                  \\end{aligned}
                  \\,,
          %p
            where now
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            is the local 1-form representative of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+connection"} spin connection
            and where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi CS
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CS_{p_2}
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+form"} Chern-Simons form
            for the second
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pontryagin+class"} Pontryagin class
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi CS
                    %mrow
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mn 2
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CS_{\frac{1}{2}p_1}
            for the first.
          %p
            That therefore not an abelian, but this
            %em nonabelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+dimensional+Chern-Simons+theory"} higher dimensional Chern-Simons theory
            should be dual to the nonabelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/6d+%282%2C0%29-superconformal+QFT"} 6d (2,0)-superconformal QFT
            was maybe first said explicitly in (
            %a{:href => "#LuWang"}> LuWang 2010
            ).
          %p
            Its gauge field is hence locally and ignoring the flux quantization subtleties a pair consisting of the abelian 3-form field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Spin+group"} Spin group
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 6
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin(6,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            (see
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supergravity+C-field"} supergravity C-field
              for global descriptions of such pairs). Or maybe rather
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Spin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 6
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Spin(6,2)
              to account for the constraint that the configurations are to be asymptotic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+spacetimes"} anti de Sitter spacetimes
              (in analogy to the well-understood situation in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/3d+quantum+gravity"} 3d quantum gravity
          %p#CommentSezginSundell
            Indeed, in
            %a{:href => "#SezginSundell"} Sezgin &amp;Sundell 2002, section 7
            more detailed arguments are given that the 7-dimensional dual to the free 6d theory is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+spin+gauge+theory"} higher spin gauge theory
            for a higher spin
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            extending the (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superconformal+group"}> super
            )
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+group"} conformal group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SO
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 6
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SO(6,2)
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbative
            description of this nonabelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/7d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 7d Chern-Simons theory
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+prequantum+field+theory"} local prequantum field theory
            (hence defined
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/non-perturbative+quantum+field+theory"} non-perturbatively
            on the global
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+%28physics%29"} fields
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+differential+string+structures"}> twisted differential string structures
            , in fact)) was discussed in (
            %a{:href => "#FSS12a"}> FSS 12a
            ,
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#FSS12b"} FSS 12b
          %p
            General discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/boundary+field+theory"} boundary
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/local+prequantum+field+theories"} local prequantum field theories
            relating higher Chern-Simons-type and higher WZW-type theories is in (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/differential+cohomology+in+a+cohesive+topos"}> dcct 13, section 3.9.14
            ). Specifically, a characterization along these lines of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green-Schwarz+action+functional"} Green-Schwarz action functional
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
            as a holographic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinity-Wess-Zumino-Witten+theory+-+contents"} higher WZW-type
            boundary theory of a 7d Chern-Simons theory is found in (
            %a{:href => "#FSS13"}> FSS 13
            ).
          %p
            Analogous discussion of the 6d theory as a higher WZW analog of a 7d Chern-Simons theory phrased in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extended+quantum+field+theory"} extended quantum field theory
            is (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/4-3-2+8-7-6"}> Freed 12
            ).
